---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy Policy: Try Convi"
  description="How Try Convi collects, uses, and protects your data."
>
  <article class="legal">
    <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p class="updated">Last updated: 20 September 2026</p>

    <p>
      This Privacy Policy explains what information Try Convi ("we", "us")
      collects, how we use it, and the choices you have. By using{" "}
      <a href="https://tryconvi.com">tryconvi.com</a> you agree to this policy.
    </p>

    <h2>Information we collect</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Email address.</strong> Only if you choose to give us one, and
        only for the thing you gave it for: either to be told when the app is
        ready, or to be reminded to practise. It is never required to use
        anything on the site, and we store it so that we can actually do what
        you asked rather than losing it the moment a send fails.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Reminders, if you ask for them.</strong> If you turn on
        reminders after clearing a section, we keep one small record: a
        notification address from your browser or your email address, which
        language you were practising, when your browser last opened the site,
        and how many reminders we have sent since. It is filed under a random
        identifier your own browser generates. There is no name and no account
        attached to it, and none of your practice progress is in it. We send at
        most four reminders before stopping on our own, and every one of them
        has a one-click way out that needs no login. That identifier is also
        what a reminder needs in order to know you have been away: while
        reminders are on, opening any page on the site tells us "this browser
        was here today", and nothing else.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Usage analytics.</strong> We use Vercel Web Analytics, which
        is privacy-first: it does not use cookies and does not track or identify
        individual visitors. It only gives us aggregate figures like page views,
        referring sites, countries, and site performance.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Site counters.</strong> We keep a handful of running totals:
        how many pages were viewed, how many separate visitors there were on a
        day, how many of those had been here before, which country they were
        in, and how many practice rounds were finished. Nothing in them
        identifies you. There is no account, no cookie and no tracking
        identifier of any kind.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>How returning visitors are counted.</strong> To know whether
        anyone comes back, your browser has to be recognisable as one we have
        seen before. Your IP address is combined with a secret value and turned
        into a one-way hash the moment your request arrives; only that hash is
        stored, and your address is never written down. The hash cannot be
        turned back into your address, we never try, and it deletes itself
        after 30 days without a visit. Your country comes from the network
        itself and is the only thing about your location that is recorded,
        never a city, never an address. This is an estimate by design: several
        people on one phone network share an address and may be counted as one
        visitor.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Local storage.</strong> Your practice progress (which stages
        you've cleared, your streak, which lines you got wrong) is kept in your
        own browser, along with your sound and voice settings and which edition
        you read last. None of it is ever sent to us. The only things that leave
        your device are the anonymous "one more round was finished" tick
        described above, which says which language deck it was and nothing else,
        and, if and only if you turned reminders on, the "this browser was
        here today" ping described above.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Technical data.</strong> We briefly process IP addresses to
        rate-limit our forms, to protect against abuse, and to produce the
        anonymous counts described above. They are never stored and never used
        to profile you.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How we use your information</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>To send you product updates and respond to your messages.</li>
      <li>
        To send the practice reminders you asked for, and to stop sending them
        when you come back, when you ask us to, or after four.
      </li>
      <li>To understand overall traffic and improve the site.</li>
      <li>To see how much the site and the practice section are actually used.</li>
      <li>To protect the service from abuse.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Payments</h2>
    <p>
      There aren't any. Try Convi is free to use and we do not take payments or
      handle card details of any kind.
    </p>

    <h2>Sharing</h2>
    <p>
      We do not sell your personal information. We share data only with the
      service providers that make Try Convi work: our email provider (Resend)
      and hosting, data infrastructure and analytics (Vercel, Upstash), and
      only as needed to run the service.
    </p>

    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      You can ask us to access or delete the personal data we hold about you at
      any time. Just email us and we'll take care of it. If you turned reminders
      on, the link at the bottom of any reminder deletes that record outright,
      immediately, without asking you anything.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Questions about this policy? Email{" "}
      <a href="mailto:hello@tryconvi.com">hello@tryconvi.com</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .legal {
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .legal h1 {
    margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
  }

  .legal .updated {
    color: var(--color-ink-soft);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .legal h2 {
    margin-top: 2rem;
    font-size: 1.25rem;
  }

  .legal a {
    color: var(--color-primary);
  }

  .legal li {
    margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
  }
</style>
